[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Generic Methods and Classes in Java"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Generics enable you to write code that can work with different types without the need for explicit casting. This leads to increased type safety and code reusability. In Java, you can create generic methods and classes."},{"order":3,"type":"heading","text":"Generic Methods"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A generic method is a method that is parameterized by one or more type parameters. These type parameters allow the method to operate on different types of objects without being rewritten for each type. The type parameters are declared within angle brackets (<>) before the return type of the method."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Declaring a Generic Method"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"The syntax for declaring a generic method is as follows:"},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"public <T> T methodName(T parameter) { 
  // Method body
  return parameter;
}"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, 'T' is the type parameter. You can use any valid identifier, but 'T' is commonly used for type parameters representing a single type.  You can have multiple type parameters, separated by commas, like `<T, U>`."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: A Generic Method to Print Array Elements"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"public class GenericMethodExample {

    public static <T> void printArray(T[] array) {
        for (T element : array) {
            System.out.print(element + " ");
        }
        System.out.println();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Integer[] intArray = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
        Double[] doubleArray = {1.1, 2.2, 3.3, 4.4};
        String[] stringArray = {"Hello", "World"};

        System.out.print("Integer Array: ");
        printArray(intArray);

        System.out.print("Double Array: ");
        printArray(doubleArray);

        System.out.print("String Array: ");
        printArray(stringArray);
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `printArray` method is generic. It accepts an array of any type 'T' and prints its elements. The main method demonstrates how to call this method with different types of arrays."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Type Inference"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"In many cases, the Java compiler can infer the type parameter based on the arguments passed to the method. This is called type inference. You don't always need to explicitly specify the type parameter when calling a generic method."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"// Explicit type specification (less common)
GenericMethodExample.<Integer>printArray(intArray);

// Type inference (more common)
printArray(intArray);"},{"order":15,"type":"heading","text":"Generic Classes"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"A generic class is a class that is parameterized by one or more type parameters. This allows the class to work with different types of objects without being rewritten for each type. The type parameters are declared within angle brackets (<>) after the class name."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Declaring a Generic Class"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"The syntax for declaring a generic class is as follows:"},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"public class ClassName<T> {
  // Class members
  private T myVariable;

  public ClassName(T myVariable) {
    this.myVariable = myVariable;
  }

  public T getMyVariable() {
    return myVariable;
  }
}"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, 'T' is the type parameter.  Any valid identifier can be used. The type parameter can be used throughout the class, including in field declarations, method parameters, and return types."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: A Generic Box Class"},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"public class Box<T> {
    private T t;

    public void set(T t) {
        this.t = t;
    }

    public T get() {
        return t;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Box<Integer> integerBox = new Box<>();
        integerBox.set(10);
        System.out.println("Integer Value: " + integerBox.get());

        Box<String> stringBox = new Box<>();
        stringBox.set("Hello");
        System.out.println("String Value: " + stringBox.get());
    }
}"},{"order":23,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `Box` class is generic. It can hold any type of object. The main method demonstrates how to create instances of the `Box` class with different types."},{"order":24,"type":"subheading","text":"Multiple Type Parameters"},{"order":25,"type":"paragraph","text":"Generic classes can have multiple type parameters. For example:"},{"order":26,"type":"code","text":"public class Pair<K, V> {
    private K key;
    private V value;

    public Pair(K key, V value) {
        this.key = key;
        this.value = value;
    }

    public K getKey() {
        return key;
    }

    public V getValue() {
        return value;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Pair<String, Integer> pair = new Pair<>("Age", 30);
        System.out.println("Key: " + pair.getKey() + ", Value: " + pair.getValue());
    }
}"},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, the `Pair` class has two type parameters, `K` and `V`, representing the key and value types, respectively."},{"order":28,"type":"heading","text":"Bounded Type Parameters"},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sometimes, you may want to restrict the types that can be used as type parameters. This can be achieved using bounded type parameters. You can specify an upper bound for a type parameter using the `extends` keyword."},{"order":30,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Bounded Type Parameter"},{"order":31,"type":"code","text":"public class NumberBox<T extends Number> {
    private T number;

    public NumberBox(T number) {
        this.number = number;
    }

    public double getDoubleValue() {
        return number.doubleValue();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        NumberBox<Integer> integerBox = new NumberBox<>(10);
        System.out.println("Double Value: " + integerBox.getDoubleValue());

        NumberBox<Double> doubleBox = new NumberBox<>(3.14);
        System.out.println("Double Value: " + doubleBox.getDoubleValue());

        // This would cause a compile-time error because String is not a Number
        // NumberBox<String> stringBox = new NumberBox<>("Hello");
    }
}"},{"order":32,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the `NumberBox` class is generic, but the type parameter `T` is bounded by `Number`. This means that only classes that extend `Number` (e.g., `Integer`, `Double`, `Float`) can be used as the type parameter. Attempting to use a type that does not extend `Number` will result in a compile-time error."},{"order":33,"type":"subheading","text":"Multiple Bounds"},{"order":34,"type":"paragraph","text":"A type parameter can have multiple bounds using the `&` operator. The first bound must be a class or interface, and subsequent bounds must be interfaces."},{"order":35,"type":"code","text":"interface Printable {
    void print();
}

interface Serializable {}

class MyClass implements Printable, Serializable {
    @Override
    public void print() {
        System.out.println("MyClass is being printed.");
    }
}

class GenericClass<T extends MyClass & Printable & Serializable> {
    private T obj;

    public GenericClass(T obj) {
        this.obj = obj;
    }

    public void process() {
        obj.print();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyClass myObject = new MyClass();
        GenericClass<MyClass> genericObject = new GenericClass<>(myObject);
        genericObject.process();
    }
}"},{"order":36,"type":"heading","text":"Wildcards"},{"order":37,"type":"paragraph","text":"Wildcards are used when you don't know the exact type parameter, or when you want to accept a range of types. There are three types of wildcards:"},{"order":38,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Unbounded Wildcard (`?`): Represents an unknown type."},{"order":39,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Upper Bounded Wildcard (`? extends Type`): Represents an unknown type that is a subtype of `Type`."},{"order":40,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Lower Bounded Wildcard (`? super Type`): Represents an unknown type that is a supertype of `Type`."},{"order":41,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Unbounded Wildcard"},{"order":42,"type":"code","text":"public static void printList(List<?> list) {
    for (Object element : list) {
        System.out.println(element);
    }
}"},{"order":43,"type":"paragraph","text":"This method can accept a list of any type. However, because the type is unknown, you can only treat the elements as `Object`."},{"order":44,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Upper Bounded Wildcard"},{"order":45,"type":"code","text":"public static double sumOfList(List<? extends Number> list) {
    double sum = 0;
    for (Number n : list) {
        sum += n.doubleValue();
    }
    return sum;
}"},{"order":46,"type":"paragraph","text":"This method can accept a list of `Number` or any of its subtypes (e.g., `Integer`, `Double`)."},{"order":47,"type":"subheading","text":"Example: Lower Bounded Wildcard"},{"order":48,"type":"code","text":"public static void addIntegers(List<? super Integer> list) {
    list.add(new Integer(1));
    list.add(new Integer(2));
}"},{"order":49,"type":"paragraph","text":"This method can accept a list of `Integer` or any of its supertypes (e.g., `Number`, `Object`). You can add `Integer` objects to the list."},{"order":50,"type":"heading","text":"Type Erasure"},{"order":51,"type":"paragraph","text":"It's important to understand that Java generics are implemented using a technique called type erasure. This means that the type parameters are erased at compile time, and the generic code is compiled into non-generic code. This ensures backward compatibility with older versions of Java that do not support generics."},{"order":52,"type":"paragraph","text":"Due to type erasure, you cannot use type parameters in certain contexts, such as creating new instances of the type parameter or using `instanceof` with the type parameter."},{"order":53,"type":"heading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":54,"type":"paragraph","text":"Generic methods and classes are powerful features in Java that allow you to write more flexible, reusable, and type-safe code. By understanding how to declare and use generic types, bounded type parameters, and wildcards, you can leverage the full potential of generics in your Java applications."}]
